Reason:
The USB core unanchors the URB from `dev->rx_submitted` before invoking the completion callback. In `kvaser_usb_read_bulk_callback`, the URB is resubmitted without being re-anchored, so it is no longer tracked in the anchor list. When `usb_kill_anchored_urbs(&dev->rx_submitted)` is called during driver removal, these completed URBs are not freed, causing a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:

usb_anchor_urb(urb, &dev->rx_submitted);
err = usb_submit_urb(urb, GFP_ATOMIC);
if (!err)
    return;
usb_unanchor_urb(urb);

Reason:
By anchoring the URB to `dev->rx_submitted` before each resubmission, the URB is again tracked in the driver’s anchor list. This ensures that `usb_kill_anchored_urbs()` can later free all URBs, including those that have been completed and resubmitted. If `usb_submit_urb()` fails, the URB is unanchored to avoid a dangling reference.

